            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things follow from the table. The hardware figures are settled: two independent
            reviews written in 2024 match Horizon’s current dimensions, weight and capacity exactly,
            which suggests the 7.8 AT has not been revised in a way that matters since. And the
            name has been used for longer than the current machine. Canadian retailers still list
            "2022" and "2023" versions of the 7.8 AT, and a 2023 review from a running site describes
            an earlier one. If you are buying from a third party or second-hand, check the SKU and
            the dimensions against the table before you rely on any of these figures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Motor Horizon Does Not Rate</h2>
<p>
            Horizon describes the 7.8 AT’s drive as a RapidSync system and its incline motor as having
            500 lb of thrust, but it does not publish a horsepower or continuous-duty rating for the
            drive motor, or for any current Horizon treadmill. Both reviews we read give 4.0 HP or
            4.0 CHP. BarBend says outright that Horizon does not list the motor size and that its
            4.0 CHP comes from its own research, alongside 3.0 CHP for the 7.0 AT and 3.5 CHP for the
            7.4 AT. A figure nobody can trace to the manufacturer is not one we print as a
            specification.
          </p>
          <p>
            That matters less than it sounds. A horsepower figure is only comparable between brands
            when both use the same basis, and many do not. The signals that are available are more
            useful. Horizon covers the motor for life, which is a direct statement of confidence in
            it. And Treadmill Review Guru timed the 7.8 AT going from 1 to 12 mph in about 19 seconds,
            against about 23.5 seconds on the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 and about 41 seconds on the
            Sole F85 in its own testing, and from 0 to 15% incline in about 20 seconds. That is quick
            for a home treadmill, and it is the reason reviewers single the Studio Series out for
            interval work. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains peak, continuous and test ratings.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same reviewer measured the incline at full stretch as 14.8% and the flat setting as
            0.9%, small differences that most home treadmills show and that do not change how you
            would train. What the published figures imply overall is a machine built for regular
            running, incline walking and intervals, with more margin than the 7.0 AT or 7.4 AT for a
            heavier runner or a household that shares it. If you want to understand why rated
            capacity and belt thickness matter more than a horsepower figure for heavy users, our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavy people</a> guide sets out the reasoning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-build" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Deck, Belt, Cushioning and Build</h2>
<p>
            The 22 by 60-inch running area is the size most runners should look for. Sixty inches of
            length leaves room for a full running stride front and back, and 22 inches of width gives
            broad runners, and anyone whose gait drifts at speed, two more inches than the common 20.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> covers the arithmetic for your height and pace.
          </p>
          <p>
            What sets the 7.8 AT apart within the Studio Series is the build around that deck. Horizon
            lists a 2.2 mm silicone belt, the thickest it uses, over a 0.8-inch (20 mm) deck, with
            60/46 mm tapered rollers and aluminium side rails, and rates the machine to 375 lb, 25 lb
            more than the 7.4 AT and 50 lb more than the 7.0 AT. A thicker belt spreads load and
            usually lasts longer under heavy use; it is one of the few parts of a treadmill that
            genuinely wears out. If you are well under the rating and run a few times a week, you are
            unlikely to notice the difference. If you are near it, or two people will run on it most
            days, you are paying for the right thing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon’s three-zone Variable Response Cushioning uses different shocks along the deck:
            softer where the foot lands, firmer where it pushes off. BarBend describes the deck as
            cushioned enough for a typical running stride; Treadmill Review Guru lists the cushioned,
            spacious deck among the machine’s strengths after more than three years of use. Neither
            describes it as bouncy. Our <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioning guide</a> explains what deck cushioning can and cannot do for your joints.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline runs from 0 to 15% with no decline. Fifteen percent leaves room for steep incline
            walking, including the <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>,
            and for hill intervals, where the quick incline response helps. If you need decline to
            train for downhill races, the 7.8 AT has none; our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a> covers the machines that do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Console, Programs, Apps and Watches</h2>
<p>
            The console is a 9.3-inch high-contrast colour TFT screen above a 16-digit LED line. It
            shows calories, distance, heart rate, incline, pace, speed and time; it is not a
            touchscreen and does not stream video. QuickDial controls on the handlebars roll forward
            and back for speed and incline — Treadmill Review Guru describes the speed dial as moving
            0.1 mph per click and the incline dial 0.5% — with custom interval keys and numbered
            quick-select buttons on the console. There is a fan, Bluetooth speakers, an audio jack,
            a USB-A charging port, multi-position tablet holders and media controls.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Programs</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s specification table names ten programs: 5K, Calorie, Custom, Custom HR,
            Distance, Fat Burn, Heart Rate, Hill Climb, Manual and Sprint 8, a 20-minute
            high-intensity interval session. Its feature list says eleven, and both reviews say ten,
            so we use ten. A Bluetooth chest strap comes in the box, which makes the heart-rate
            programs usable from day one; the handrail grips are there too, but BarBend notes that <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>pulse grips</a> are less accurate than a strap.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No subscription, open apps</h3>
          <p>
            Nothing on the 7.8 AT needs a membership. It sends live speed, incline and distance over
            Bluetooth FTMS, and Horizon names Peloton, Zwift and Kinomap, plus syncing with Strava and
            other tracking apps. Those apps carry their own fees, and they run on your own phone or
            tablet, not on the console. Note what FTMS does here: the apps read the treadmill’s data
            but do not control it, so when a class calls for a change, you turn the dial yourself.
            Treadmill Review Guru reports that the included strap works with the Peloton app. If
            automatic control is what keeps you training, a NordicTrack built around iFIT is designed
            for that; our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill roundup</a> sets out the trade-off, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a> covers app running in more depth.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Watches</h3>
          <p>
            One thing the flagship does not do: Horizon’s table lists no <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apple-watch/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Apple Watch</a>, Apple GymKit or
            Samsung Galaxy Watch pairing on the 7.8 AT. Among current Horizons, only the <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">7.0 AT</a> lists
            them. If keeping treadmill distance and pace in your watch’s records matters to you, that
            is a real point in the cheaper machine’s favour, and the 7.8 AT’s chest strap does not
            replace it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owner-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Independent Reviews Consistently Report</h2>
<p>
            We list a point here only when it appears in both of the independent reviews we read, or
            when one review measured it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fast speed and incline changes.</strong> Both reviews highlight the QuickDial controls for intervals and HIIT; one timed 1 to 12 mph at about 19 seconds and 0 to 15% at about 20 seconds.</li>
            <li><strong>A sturdy, durable frame.</strong> Both describe it as built for hard training; one has used it for more than three years.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy and hard to move.</strong> Both call out the 330 lb weight. It folds and has transport wheels, but it is not a machine you move casually.</li>
            <li><strong>A weak fan.</strong> BarBend’s tester found the fan lacked power; Treadmill Review Guru says it is small and placed low enough to blow in a taller user’s face.</li>
            <li><strong>No built-in streaming.</strong> Both note that the screen shows metrics only, so classes run on your own device.</li>
            <li><strong>Noise.</strong> One review measured 46.2 dB at 3 mph rising to 65.6 dB at 12 mph, comparing it with background music at walking pace and a window fan on high at speed.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Points made in only one review — that the console looks dated, that the side handles
            could be longer, that the 7.8 AT is essentially the 7.4 AT with a bigger motor — are worth
            knowing but are not patterns. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a> explains how to tell one from the other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-power-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Folding, Space, Power and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            Assembled, the 7.8 AT is 76 inches long, 37 wide and 64 tall, about 19.5 square feet.
            Folded with its one-step hydraulic assist it is 42 inches long and 71 tall, a little under
            11 square feet. It weighs 330 lb, so although it rolls on transport wheels, plan the
            delivery route carefully: Horizon gives the box as 78 by 40 by 13.5 inches and about
            391 lb. Our <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to moving a treadmill</a> covers stairs and doorways.
          </p>
          <p>
            For ceiling height, add the step-on height of 8 3/4 inches to the tallest user’s height,
            then a margin for the bounce of running and the rise of the deck at full incline.
            Treadmill Review Guru suggests roughly an 8.5-foot ceiling for a six-foot runner doing
            high-intensity work. In a basement with beams or ducts, measure the lowest point above
            where you will stand. Our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space guide</a> covers clearance behind the deck as well.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon specifies a 120 V, 60 Hz supply and says the 7.8 AT is not compatible with GFCI
            outlets. Garages and basements often have GFCI outlets, so check before delivery, and do
            not run it from an extension lead or surge strip. Our <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">circuit guide</a> explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            Delivery options at checkout are standard (a two-person crew to the main entrance, porch,
            garage or driveway, in select areas), room of choice, and white glove with full assembly and
            packaging removal, with scheduled, signature-on-delivery service; not every option is
            available for every address. Horizon’s page says orders leave the warehouse within one to
            five days — it gives both figures — and may then be in transit for 10 to 15 days, with
            weekday delivery windows. When BarBend checked in 2024 it paid $139 for standard shipping
            and listed in-room delivery at $219 and delivery with assembly at $349; on 5 November 2026
            Horizon was advertising free shipping on Studio Series treadmills until 14 November, and
            the extra-cost options were priced at checkout. Horizon offers a 30-day guarantee with a
            full refund; read its store policy on return shipping before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Horizon 7.8 AT Price and How to Judge a Sale (as of 5 November 2026)</h2>
<p>
            Horizon prices the 7.8 AT the way it prices the rest of the range: a high reference price
            that it does not charge, a sale price that it does, and periodic codes and shipping offers
            on top. Here is what we saw on 5 November 2026 and how to read it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Horizon prices the 7.8 AT</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The reference price is not the selling price.</strong> Horizon shows $2,699 as the regular price and $1,999 as the sale price. The $1,999 is what it charges day to day, so a discount measured against $2,699 tells you nothing.</li>
            <li><strong>Codes come and go.</strong> On 13 October 2026 our <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Horizon treadmills</a> guide recorded the 7.8 AT at $1,999, or $1,899 with a code. On 5 November we saw no code price, only a free-shipping offer on Studio Series treadmills ending 14 November.</li>
            <li><strong>Delivery is part of the price.</strong> Free standard shipping is worth having, but room-of-choice and white-glove delivery are charged at checkout, and they are the options most buyers of a 330 lb machine end up wanting.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to judge a deal on the 7.8 AT</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Compare with the best code price, not the reference price.</strong> The useful benchmark is the lowest delivered price you have seen recently — $1,899 with a code in October. An offer is a real saving only if it beats that once delivery is added.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the 7.4 AT in view.</strong> If a sale narrows or widens the $200 gap between the two, the decision changes. At equal discounts the 7.4 AT is the better value for most runners.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the exact model.</strong> The current SKU is HTM1381-01. Third-party and second-hand listings sometimes carry the 2022 or 2023 versions, whose figures may differ.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the ship date.</strong> Horizon shows ship dates on its product pages when stock is short; on 13 October Horizon showed the 7.8 AT shipping the week of 26 October.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask about price changes after purchase.</strong> We did not find a published price-adjustment policy. If you buy before a sale and the price drops within your 30-day guarantee, ask Horizon what it will do, and get the answer in writing.</li>
            <li><strong>Cross-shop the same day.</strong> On 5 November Sole listed the F85 at $2,199.99 and Horizon listed the 7.4 AT at $1,799. A deal is only a deal relative to the alternatives on the same day.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill Black Friday guide</a> covers how sale events work across brands, and <a href="/best-treadmills-under-2000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our under-$2,000 guide</a> sets out the rest of the field at this price.
          </p>
        </div>

