              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price seen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,799</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,099</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,199.99</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>7.8 AT vs 7.4 AT.</strong> This is the decision most 7.8 AT buyers are really
            making. Deck size, speed, incline, controls, app support, the included strap and the
            warranty are the same. The 7.8 AT adds 25 lb of rated capacity, a thicker belt, aluminium
            side rails and a colour screen for $200 more at the sale prices we saw. Pay it if you
            weigh more than about 300 lb, the machine will be shared by two regular runners, or you
            will run most days for years; that is where the extra margin earns its keep. Otherwise
            the 7.4 AT is the better value, and BarBend’s description of the 7.8 AT as essentially the
            same treadmill with a bigger motor is a fair summary.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>7.0 AT.</strong> At $1,099 on the same day, the 7.0 AT is $900 cheaper. It has a
            20-inch belt, a 325 lb rating and three years of parts with one of labour, and it is the
            only Horizon that pairs with an Apple or Samsung watch. Runners of average build who do
            not need the wider deck should read our <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT review</a> before spending more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F85.</strong> The F85 is $200 more than the 7.8 AT and offers a 15.6-inch
            touchscreen and a published 3.5 HP motor rating, on the same deck size and incline range.
            The Horizon has the higher weight rating and two more years of parts cover with one more
            of labour. Our <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F85 review</a> sets out the versions sold under that name, and our <a href="/bowflex-t9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T9 review</a> covers the sister brand’s nearest equivalent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Warranty, Read Properly</h2>
<p>
            The 7.8 AT carries a lifetime warranty on the frame and motor, five years on parts and two
            years on labour for home use, as published on Horizon’s product page and confirmed by both
            independent reviews. Lifetime motor cover is the answer to the missing horsepower figure,
            and five years of parts is the main thing the Studio Series’ upper two models buy over the
            7.0 AT, which has three. BarBend also reported optional paid extensions from Horizon in
            2024; check what is offered at checkout, and what it adds, before paying for one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two years of labour is the term to plan around: from year three, a covered part may arrive
            free while the visit to fit it does not. Many treadmill parts are within reach of a
            careful owner with the manual, but some jobs are awkward on a 330 lb machine. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a> explains how to read the split. Horizon’s terms are for home use, and its manuals tie the
            warranty to correct use — the right electrical supply and the maintenance schedule — so
            keep a simple log of when you clean and check the belt; our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance guide</a> sets out a schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            Safety record: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database on
            5 November 2026 and found no recall of the 7.8 AT. Horizon’s only treadmill recall in
            recent years is the October 2022 recall of T101-05 units for a fall hazard, covered in our <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101 review</a> and in <a href="/is-horizon-a-good-treadmill-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our Horizon brand assessment</a>,
            which also covers how the parent company, Johnson Health Tech, handles service. Our <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon Fitness guide</a> sets out the whole range and the company behind it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the Horizon 7.8 AT</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavy runner or a heavy user.</strong> A 375 lb rating, a
            2.2 mm belt and five years of parts cover on a 22 by 60-inch deck are the reasons the
            7.8 AT exists, and they are worth paying for if you are near the 7.4 AT’s 350 lb limit or
            you will run on it most days.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if the machine will be shared.</strong> Two regular runners double the wear
            on a belt and deck. The extra margin is cheap insurance against that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the 7.4 AT instead</strong> if you are of ordinary build and run a few times a
            week. You get the same deck, speed, incline and warranty for less, and you will not miss
            the colour screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the 7.0 AT</strong> if you want Apple or Samsung watch pairing or a lower price,
            and a 20-inch belt suits you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you want classes to change the speed and incline for you,
            need decline, or want a touchscreen; the Sole F85 and iFIT-based NordicTrack machines are
            the obvious alternatives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it at the sale or code price.</strong> Check the delivered total and the ship
            date, and test the strap and app combination you plan to use within the 30-day window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Horizon 7.8 AT</h2>
          <p>The 7.8 AT is a straightforward machine to buy well. These are the errors that cost buyers money or frustration.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring a deal against the $2,699 reference price</h3>
              <p>Horizon sells the 7.8 AT at its sale price every day, and codes sometimes take it lower. A discount quoted from $2,699 is not a saving. Compare against the best code price you have seen and the delivered total.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for the 7.8 AT when the 7.4 AT would do</h3>
              <p>The two share their deck, speed, incline and warranty. If you are well under 350 lb and run a few times a week, the extra capacity and thicker belt are margin you will not use.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting watch pairing on the flagship</h3>
              <p>Horizon lists Apple Watch, GymKit and Samsung Galaxy Watch pairing only on the 7.0 AT. If that matters, check the specification table before you choose the top model.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Putting it on a GFCI outlet in a garage or basement</h3>
              <p>Horizon says the 7.8 AT is not GFCI-compatible. Check the outlet before delivery day, and do not use an extension lead or surge strip, because both are common causes of a treadmill that cuts out.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The Horizon Fitness 7.8 AT is the Horizon for heavy users and heavy use</strong>:
            a 22 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline, a 375 lb rating, a 2.2 mm belt, open app support and a
            lifetime frame and motor warranty with five years on parts and two on labour, at a sale
            price of $1,999 on 5 November 2026. Horizon does not publish its motor rating.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most runners of ordinary build the <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">7.4 AT</a> does the same job for $200 less. Buy the 7.8 AT at the sale or code price, never the
            reference price, and price the Sole F85 next to it on the day.
          </p>
        </div>

