        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the Horizon 7.8 AT treadmill good?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On its published figures and two independent reviews, yes, especially for heavy users and high mileage. It has a 22 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a 375 lb rating, a 2.2 mm belt and lifetime frame and motor cover with five years on parts and two on labour, and needs no subscription. Horizon does not publish a motor rating. We have not tested it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the Horizon Fitness 7.8 Studio treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is the same machine. The 7.8 AT is the top model in Horizon’s Studio Series, above the 7.0 AT and 7.4 AT, and Horizon lists it as the 7.8 AT Treadmill, SKU HTM1381-01. Listings that say "7.8 Studio" or "7.8AT" refer to it, though older 2022 and 2023 versions are still on some retailer sites.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What motor does the Horizon 7.8 AT have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Horizon describes a RapidSync drive and a 500 lb-thrust incline motor but publishes no horsepower rating. Reviews quote 4.0 HP or 4.0 CHP, and one says the figure comes from its own research. The lifetime motor warranty and a measured 1 to 12 mph time of about 19 seconds are more reliable signals than an unofficial number.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Horizon 7.8 AT vs 7.4 AT: which should I buy?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Both have a 22 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a chest strap and the same lifetime, five-year parts and two-year labour warranty. The 7.8 AT adds a 375 lb rating against 350 lb, a thicker belt, aluminium side rails and a colour screen for $200 more at the prices we saw on 5 November 2026. Heavy, shared or daily use justifies it; otherwise the 7.4 AT is better value.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Horizon 7.8 AT need a subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Its ten programs, dials and display work on their own. It sends speed, incline and distance over Bluetooth FTMS to apps such as Peloton, Zwift and Kinomap on your own device, which have their own fees, but the apps read the data rather than control the machine, so you set speed and incline yourself.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Horizon 7.8 AT work with Apple Watch?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Horizon’s specification table lists no Apple Watch, Apple GymKit or Samsung Galaxy Watch pairing on the 7.8 AT. Among current Horizons only the 7.0 AT lists them. The 7.8 AT includes a Bluetooth chest strap, and it pairs with training apps over Bluetooth FTMS.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
